The C is only ~10 lines, so the translation work was in the details rather than the structure:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m is reimplemented over a one-byte-pushback stdin reader: it skips all [38;5;10misspace[0m bytes (so the number can be on any later line), takes an optional sign, then digits, and ungets the terminating byte. [38;5;10mmain[0m ignores scanf's return value, so on EOF, whitespace-only, or non-numeric input [38;5;10mx[0m stays [38;5;10m0[0m and the program prints [38;5;10m300[0m — preserved rather than "fixed."[0m[0m
- Overflow follows glibc's actual conversion: accumulate in an [38;5;10mi64[0m that saturates at [38;5;10mi64::MIN[0m/[38;5;10mi64::MAX[0m, then store the low 32 bits. That's why [38;5;10m"99999999999999999999999"[0m prints [38;5;10m298[0m in both (saturated [38;5;10mi64::MAX[0m truncates to [38;5;10m-1[0m), and it would be wrong with naive saturation to [38;5;10mi32[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m2*x + 300[0m uses [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m. This is signed-overflow UB in C, but matches what gcc emits; verified at the wrap boundary ([38;5;10m1073741674[0m vs [38;5;10m1073741675[0m).[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mauto int y[0m is just C's default storage class — no Rust equivalent needed.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One caveat worth stating: the two overflow behaviors above match glibc on x86-64 Linux with gcc, which is what I tested against. They are UB or implementation-defined in C, so a different libc or compiler could diverge on those specific inputs; all in-range inputs are unambiguous.[0m[0m
